@@ -0,0 +1,146 @@
+# Test suite performance notes
+
+These timings are intended for like-for-like comparisons. Use the same RSpec seed and keep PostgreSQL and Redis available. Run one spec directory at a time; do not include system or streaming specs.
+
+## Models phase (2026-11-23)
+
+Baseline command:
+
+```sh
+bundle exec rspec spec/models --format progress --profile 30 --seed 1621
+```
+
+The baseline on this CI-like container contained 1,795 examples, finished with no failures, and took:
+
+- 214.0 seconds of RSpec execution (3:34)
+- 216 seconds wall time
+
+The optimized run uses the same command and seed. It still contains 1,795 examples, finishes with no failures, and takes:
+
+- 135.9 seconds of RSpec execution (2:15.9)
+- 140 seconds wall time
+- 36.5% less RSpec execution time, or 35.2% less wall time (1.57x faster by RSpec time)
+
+### What was measured
+
+TestProf EventProf attributed 66.571 seconds (28.6% of its profiled run) to 3,908 synchronous Sidekiq jobs:
+
+```sh
+EVENT_PROF=sidekiq.inline \
+  EVENT_PROF_TOP=25 \
+  EVENT_PROF_EXAMPLES=1 \
+  FPROF=1 \
+  bundle exec rspec spec/models --require test_prof --format progress
+```
+
+The jobs came from model callbacks even though worker execution is covered by the separate worker specs. Model examples use Sidekiq's fake queue. At the end of that phase, other spec types still retained inline execution; the request phase below narrows that exception. Active Job uses its test adapter around model examples so mail-capture assertions still execute the intended delivery jobs.
+
+RSpecDissect also showed that repeated setup dominated the optimized suite before fixture sharing (115.344 seconds of setup out of 144.813 seconds):
+
+```sh
+RD_PROF=1 RD_PROF_TOP=20 RD_PROF_LET_TOP=8 \
+  bundle exec rspec spec/models --require test_prof --format progress --seed 1621
+```
+
+The largest setup groups included:
+
+| Group | Repeated setup | Examples |
+| --- | ---: | ---: |
+| AccountStatusesCleanupPolicy | 11.234s | 36 |
+| TagFeed | 10.586s | 33 |
+| Trends::Statuses | 10.295s | 12 |
+| Status | 7.861s | 62 |
+| Notification | 6.095s | 25 |
+| MediaAttachment | 5.940s | 27 |
+| PublicFeed | 5.486s | 43 |
+
+Stable fixture graphs in `AccountStatusesCleanupPolicy` and `TagFeed` now use `let_it_be(..., reload: true)`. The cleanup policy's repeated relationship setup uses `before_all`. Reloads and transactional examples keep the shared records isolated while avoiding per-example reconstruction.
+
+No application code, factories, request specs, system specs, or streaming specs were changed during this phase.
+
+## Requests phase (2026-11-23)
+
+Baseline command:
+
+```sh
+bundle exec rspec spec/requests --format progress --profile 30 --seed 1621
+```
+
+The pre-optimization baseline contained 1,877 examples, finished with no failures, and took:
+
+- 220.9 seconds of RSpec execution (3:40.9)
+- 225 seconds wall time
+
+The optimized run uses the same command and seed. It still contains 1,877 examples, finishes with no failures, and takes:
+
+- 147.3 seconds of RSpec execution (2:27.3)
+- 151 seconds wall time
+- 33.3% less RSpec execution time, or 32.9% less wall time (1.50x faster by RSpec time)
+
+A randomized isolation check without profiling, using seed 9473, passed the same 1,877 examples in 111.38 seconds (114 seconds wall time).
+
+### What was measured
+
+The baseline EventProf run attributed 81.646 seconds (32.25% of the profiled 253.135-second run) to 7,592 synchronous Sidekiq jobs:
+
+```sh
+EVENT_PROF=sidekiq.inline \
+  EVENT_PROF_TOP=25 \
+  EVENT_PROF_EXAMPLES=1 \
+  FPROF=1 \
+  bundle exec rspec spec/requests --require test_prof --format progress --seed 1621
+```
+
+Request examples now use Sidekiq's fake queue by default. The two unfavourite contexts that assert the worker's database effect immediately retain inline execution with `inline_sidekiq` metadata. Active Job uses its test adapter around request examples so mail-capture assertions still execute delivery jobs.
+
+Setups which need a particular worker side effect drain only that worker after enqueueing it. This materializes notification, conversation, and feed fixtures without also executing unrelated link crawl, delivery, streaming, and notification jobs. Notification setup preserves the original enqueue order before sharing the resulting records with `let_it_be` and `before_all`.
+
+The common API authentication context now shares and reloads its user while keeping tokens per example, because nested examples override OAuth scopes. `let_it_be(..., reload: true)` also shares the cache matrix fixtures and stable ActivityPub fixture graphs.
+
+Before fixture sharing, RSpecDissect measured 39.809 seconds of setup for `Caching behavior` across 258 examples. Afterward, its request-specific setup report is 8.780 seconds. The final profiled request run spent 100.706 seconds of its 157.964 seconds in setup; the largest remaining groups were cache requests (8.780s), public timelines (4.275s), ActivityPub contexts (4.004s), link timelines (3.905s), and ActivityPub replies (3.611s).
+
+No application code, factories, system specs, or streaming specs were changed during this phase.
+
+## Factory phase (2026-11-23)
+
+Run one directory at a time with the same seed used by the configuration phases:
+
+```sh
+FPROF=1 bundle exec rspec spec/models --require test_prof --format progress --seed 1621
+FPROF=1 bundle exec rspec spec/requests --require test_prof --format progress --seed 1621
+```
+
+Before this phase, the `status` fabricator created a bookmark after every persisted status, and every persisted `user` fabricator (including role variants) created two login activities. These records were not part of the assertions in the affected examples.
+
+| Profiled group | Before: examples / factories / RSpec time | After: examples / factories / RSpec time | Time reduction |
+| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
+| Models | 1,795 / 4,041 / 131.5s | 1,795 / 3,034 / 79.84s | 39.3% |
+| Requests | 1,877 / 5,760 / 120.3s | 1,877 / 3,822 / 107.19s | 10.9% |
+| Combined | 3,672 / 9,801 / 251.8s | 3,672 / 6,856 / 187.03s | 25.7% |
+
+The model profile previously contained 741 bookmarks, of which only 4 were explicit top-level uses, plus 270 login activities created entirely as factory callbacks. The request profile contained 639 bookmarks, of which only 9 were explicit, plus 1,308 callback-created login activities. After removing the callbacks, the model profile made only the 4 explicit bookmarks and the request profile made only the 9 explicit bookmarks; neither group made implicit login activities.
+
+FactoryProf's nested totals overlap. In particular, the models profile's `status` cost fell from 48.81 seconds at 0.0661 seconds per call to 16.32 seconds at 0.0221 seconds per call because the bookmark graph was no longer recorded inside every status fabrication.
+
+The final non-profiler runs used the original benchmark commands and seed:
+
+```sh
+bundle exec rspec spec/models --format progress --profile 30 --seed 1621
+bundle exec rspec spec/requests --format progress --profile 30 --seed 1621
+```
+
+- Models: 86.02 seconds RSpec execution and 89 seconds wall time, with 1,795 examples and no failures.
+- Requests: 97.59 seconds RSpec execution and 101 seconds wall time, with 1,877 examples and no failures.
+- Combined: 183.61 seconds RSpec execution and 190 seconds wall time.
+
+Compared with the pre-factory configuration-phase result (283.2 seconds RSpec execution and 291 seconds wall time), this phase reduced RSpec time by 35.2% and wall time by 34.7%. Compared with the original 434.9-second RSpec baseline and 441-second wall baseline, both groups together are 2.37x faster by RSpec time and 2.32x faster by wall time. No spec assertions, application code, system specs, or streaming specs changed in this phase.
+
+The factory rule for future work is to keep a default fabricator to the minimum valid record graph. Side-effect records should be requested explicitly in the example or made opt-in through a trait; an `after_create` callback on a common fabricator multiplies that cost across every caller.
+
+## Follow-up opportunities
+
+- The 2x target is met. Use these 183.61-second combined and seed-specific runs as the next like-for-like baseline.
+- The largest remaining model factories are `account` (1,160 calls) and `status` (738 calls); the largest remaining request factories are `accessible_access_token` (909), `account` (704), and `status` (630). FactoryProf totals overlap, so profile a concrete change instead of summing these times.
+- Further factory work should first determine whether stable associations can be supplied from shared `let_it_be` fixtures. Avoid changing password, key, media, or callback defaults without checking behavior-specific specs.
+- Remaining high-cost setup can be revisited with RSpecDissect in `Trends::Statuses`, `Status`, `MediaAttachment`, public/link timelines, and signature verification requests. Preserve per-example reload isolation around mutable shared records.
+- Keep a second seed when introducing shared state. This factory phase changes defaults only and the same-seed model and request runs both remain isolated and green.
